#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124177 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124178 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124179 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e88a357-c5cc-5568-85ae-94bdda9b4ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362333} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdb3595-7a0a-53db-81b3-618eb321a9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362334} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6c0613-d577-5ca5-be23-3df167fde053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362335} {:answer/id #uuid "8a613c31-f47a-5e9c-9110-8be6de36c91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362336} {:answer/id #uuid "5e58f2ec-e905-5c15-8ed1-3bdd36b6c30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a613c31-f47a-5e9c-9110-8be6de36c91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362336} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3b339c-645a-5aca-94cb-877c4133c408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fdbef4a-96b4-5d61-a423-a5d3b28ba07f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130257" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "687ee922-7345-5c51-9050-5eb5519ffb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362340} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2019ec-e80f-557c-be6d-c6b58ef95b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362341} {:answer/id #uuid "9c018086-5c23-5a8f-8b15-232c9a5c3892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342} {:answer/id #uuid "3df1bb49-be12-579a-be49-02acee544c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362343} {:answer/id #uuid "1387ecfa-c811-5dbd-89f9-1d56855c9c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687ee922-7345-5c51-9050-5eb5519ffb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362340} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e33e530-7e03-562a-abfe-728c1d513687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1939235-4888-56a0-832d-3d89089852b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130260" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,-1) & =∇\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5af4623-be33-50ee-9b8b-affeaaa1f686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362347} {:answer/id #uuid "e01a7e7c-215a-5c91-9229-70341f5ab71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348} {:answer/id #uuid "26f64e91-56bc-5937-81a1-991402d48081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362349} {:answer/id #uuid "543ca7e2-dae8-5a16-9398-3524d45062ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362350} {:answer/id #uuid "e11e3381-95c5-5142-9929-7f7bc8f65b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e11e3381-95c5-5142-9929-7f7bc8f65b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362351} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27610ab-136e-50ea-b6f9-559952982e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6ac9420-587f-5958-bf51-cf604553d217" :question/math-academy-id "q-130263" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i-j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\,i+(2(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e20bb8d1-4a27-53ab-bf11-ee0040b4d41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354} {:answer/id #uuid "547282ff-028e-55c8-a319-604d2cad118a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362355} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f19765-8d7a-5f8e-aa4a-c3bfd8539b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362356} {:answer/id #uuid "29b174ae-4cbc-5631-8056-f1ecb81bac51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362357} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4ed1dd-5d74-5f08-8bfb-0f4faee446fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29b174ae-4cbc-5631-8056-f1ecb81bac51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362357} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37980df-1328-5f31-80ff-f01129ef7dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fca48a5-8fc2-501c-980a-097a661d54fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130276" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c797a65e-afbd-5b9c-af24-7c12ee36fbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362361} {:answer/id #uuid "a0135bfd-5aa4-5e54-8c91-06a1436467b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362362} {:answer/id #uuid "94303edc-c45c-5675-a475-72c88e4fbfc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362363} {:answer/id #uuid "88dc4d39-4bfc-56d4-90e6-65415aa09257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362364} {:answer/id #uuid "8df04b1f-42b0-57ad-8752-d0505d9bb43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8df04b1f-42b0-57ad-8752-d0505d9bb43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362365} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7afa458-96db-5d55-9c98-3c6f654f4d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62433894-eb6d-5153-9489-351a9fe549d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130801" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b763a5bc-0950-5164-8a91-0604e9e6c664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362368} {:answer/id #uuid "fad4549b-319c-57d3-a044-00eb4e2c7649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362369} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b2bc67-f6fa-526b-965f-d373f0f9d9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362370} {:answer/id #uuid "86ed191c-8281-514c-a6a7-5bcbdb09338e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362371} {:answer/id #uuid "0b92314b-f854-572d-a3ff-6fd7cdcc9773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b92314b-f854-572d-a3ff-6fd7cdcc9773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362372} :answer-field/id #uuid "f497ec9e-3844-5d63-bf10-6ec8e5165911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b51eb40-d88a-5bba-aeaf-ddb201f3cc1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36290" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4a9e24-005a-5dfb-8480-4713eb0fefba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362375} {:answer/id #uuid "535f9046-a320-57a6-a8a3-35c141a42407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362376} {:answer/id #uuid "269b3df3-6718-5e56-95a7-04bec75742f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362377} {:answer/id #uuid "da493b2b-9d48-57e0-82ee-bdf854cc43cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362378} {:answer/id #uuid "b6290e81-7555-5c3a-a4d9-f162c0d81c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "269b3df3-6718-5e56-95a7-04bec75742f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362377} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f126513-a801-56d0-a2c4-55b140281628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d871812-574c-5223-8f52-1d1e692d14a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-36300" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee76bc59-b143-5bac-b431-bb48abc4fd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362381} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7976da-3265-5067-a1bb-1e75f8cf9809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362382} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3b04a1-94ab-5c54-b6ee-a40a08dc463f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362383} {:answer/id #uuid "11caf6ea-e88e-501b-8824-ddb49cff3a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362384} {:answer/id #uuid "42f3c25b-57f9-54fc-89b9-4e1d4ba6655c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11caf6ea-e88e-501b-8824-ddb49cff3a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362384} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b43372-9a9f-5d8d-8b56-c1ea0046ec64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4555297-09f5-5912-a47c-1297a4cf0890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362388} {:answer/id #uuid "c96d5d44-8cf0-5fb9-9852-354e11fb166d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362389} {:answer/id #uuid "bbeda845-ac3d-5c75-8aab-307df77aabaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,0,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362390} {:answer/id #uuid "e709157c-c442-55e7-8de1-60b30f34941e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362391} {:answer/id #uuid "60397245-d895-528a-a207-7574581256a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,-\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4555297-09f5-5912-a47c-1297a4cf0890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362388} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d1aea48-80f6-5016-90d2-19ee585adba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "043c94bf-7651-58ea-abe2-4c89798a1721" :question/math-academy-id "q-37080" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x$, in what direction does the function increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y),\\,6xz⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0)+3\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0),\\,6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4323eae-5ac4-58e8-b41d-627a420ef2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395} {:answer/id #uuid "d86a247c-1534-5a30-876a-7f6322464c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362396} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf6aae3-1138-5d90-8eb9-c165d7f56024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362397} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5f7754-33dd-530f-9750-dfe41fde0328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362398} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4ac6bc-381a-509a-b61f-97b7c35e4832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4323eae-5ac4-58e8-b41d-627a420ef2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa780bc-b03f-5573-8318-518efa9363ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90691e53-4061-50e3-bc0b-ef8fb637c9f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a140e9d-79e3-5459-9b83-26f805a4c62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e+1)\\text{ }i+(e-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362402} {:answer/id #uuid "c247b874-197c-5a17-830b-7cbb40ddf139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362403} {:answer/id #uuid "368da0de-e1e4-5a0a-922c-b0744e7b3980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404} {:answer/id #uuid "1c992aaf-eb07-5439-8247-2736c78df2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{-1}-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362405} {:answer/id #uuid "315e95ef-eba2-5540-acb6-0190ee0baffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\text{ }i+ej" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "368da0de-e1e4-5a0a-922c-b0744e7b3980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ef3c1f-3556-55d4-9634-ffe5bc370834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "908c4bea-443b-5920-870d-507ba8f31d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-83554" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0291e86d-e6ef-51c5-8cd8-beaa672aa63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362409} {:answer/id #uuid "b300e76e-dac5-5178-8d06-57c2b767e0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362410} {:answer/id #uuid "6def077c-81d2-5fff-bdc9-37b29fbcf404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362411} {:answer/id #uuid "d74e46a2-b98e-5792-a519-12a731e04560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362412} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6dbd1c-86e4-529c-8380-ae91f6ffbe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc6dbd1c-86e4-529c-8380-ae91f6ffbe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362413} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b270d00-5f3c-5636-9b99-f29ddea55a3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26233936-7690-5f63-84cf-a5a4e6f7c30f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83608" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-2,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2169d0-f362-5b0f-8c3d-26181fbd68c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362416} {:answer/id #uuid "db68217b-436e-5bec-9776-965472658894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362417} {:answer/id #uuid "c5044a50-4447-5400-b459-f9317a49bc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362418} {:answer/id #uuid "eb266df5-9daf-5687-906c-193075a88abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362419} {:answer/id #uuid "3023fb85-7f8e-557f-8b4a-2923ca99c63f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db68217b-436e-5bec-9776-965472658894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362417} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f825ffc-8232-57b0-90f2-fed9245285e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87567443-ec6a-5071-9f0c-ac11c5afa2b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83613" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-1,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{sec}^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300295cf-793a-54dc-a310-4a8da64bd4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362423} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6750da-36d0-5d28-9047-0ce97ce6d873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362424} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1c9334-d068-5fb9-aa88-33222857c6bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362425} {:answer/id #uuid "04d646ef-da42-535b-a5ff-b181b7439405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362426} {:answer/id #uuid "89814ebb-f414-5f1e-a114-cee243d6e163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6750da-36d0-5d28-9047-0ce97ce6d873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362424} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e9d5f6-de4b-580b-b681-7ef6048c7e02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f0245e-794a-58d1-963e-1b63286cc3b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86429" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3741d2da-e14f-58c2-8390-902b62b40ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362430} {:answer/id #uuid "c22f01c3-dae7-542c-b484-0de6318f8a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362431} {:answer/id #uuid "017f6928-a034-5b9c-bb5e-035eb7ced53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362432} {:answer/id #uuid "0b267b3e-5465-59f4-8c3c-10e417b6cf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362433} {:answer/id #uuid "1446c9fe-1260-5423-8ae4-551a8950f52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b267b3e-5465-59f4-8c3c-10e417b6cf5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362433} :answer-field/id #uuid "186f9b8d-5bd1-5bbc-a54a-0613a65d8d21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e1c6515-4694-552d-8e95-18dee2c7d022" :question/math-academy-id "q-86537" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$"}]}
